fix: tighten the mobile hero and enlarge its carousel

The headline sat low with a band of empty texture above and below it,
and the photo strip was pushed toward the fold at a size where the
images read as thumbnails.

Three things were holding the space. The section centred its type
against a full-screen height, so the words floated in the middle; it
now starts from the top with no height floor on a phone, so the section
is only as tall as the type and its padding. And the content wrapper
carried pb-[clamp(3rem,10vh,7rem)] — on a tall phone that 10vh resolved
to 102px, which was most of the gap on its own. That padding is now
scoped to md and up, where it was designed for.

Measured at 425px, the distance from the headline to the strip falls
from 355px to 48px.

The carousel cells are shares of the row calibrated for six across a
desktop; the same shares left each around 80px on a phone. A
--cell-scale multiplier widens them below md, taking the first cell from
80px to 153px. The row already overflows and scrolls, so wider cells
simply mean fewer visible at once, which is the right trade on a narrow
screen. Scale was set to 2.1 first and eased to 1.55 — at 2.1 the cells
were large enough to crop faces badly.

Desktop is untouched: the wrapper keeps its 90px padding and the cells
their original shares.
